## Overview

ME 536 (Computational Intelligence in Control Engineering) is a revamped technical elective covering reinforcement learning,
deep RL, neural networks, and state inference, culminating in an open-ended robotics project in NVIDIA Isaac Sim rather than a traditional exam.
The course is designed to connect theory to application while building entrepreneurial mindset habits — particularly creating value through stakeholder-driven,
open-ended design. The primary obstacle to the final project is Isaac Sim's steep learning curve and GPU demands, which this effort addresses by adapting an
existing research-grade codesign interface into a streamlined course tool accessible to undergraduates.

## Methods

- Course content is developed across reinforcement learning (tabular, function approximation, deep RL), neural network architectures,
and model-based state inference, with labs emphasizing real-world control problems over clean theoretical benchmarks.
- An existing research-grade Isaac Sim codesign interface is adapted into a course-accessible tool, 
lowering the barrier to entry for students working with GPU-accelerated simulation and external algorithm integration.
- Students complete an open-ended final project in Isaac Sim, selecting robot and sensor designs, engineering reward structures, 
and developing intelligent controllers toward a self-defined, stakeholder-grounded objective.



